    {
      "id": "library/10665-258517/blocks/prose-sec-000",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "INTRODUCTION",
      "pageStart": 4,
      "pageEnd": 6,
      "target": "../sections/sec-000-introduction.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Following the WHO recommendation in September 2015 that “oral pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP) should be offered as \nan additional prevention choice for people at substantial risk of HIV infection as part of combination HIV prevention \napproaches”, partners in countries expressed the need for practical advice on how to consider the introduction of PrEP and \nstart implementation. In response, WHO has developed this series of modules to support the implementation of PrEP \namong a range of populations in different settings. \nAlthough there is growing acknowledgement of PrEP’s potential as an addit",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Introduces WHO's series of PrEP implementation modules, developed after the 2015 recommendation that oral PrEP be offered to people at substantial risk of HIV. It notes that PrEP should not displace established prevention methods, that key populations may face legal and social barriers, and that individuals decide whether to use it. It describes the audience, guiding principles and the content of each module.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-258517/blocks/prose-sec-001",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "THE COUNSELLORS MODULE",
      "pageStart": 6,
      "pageEnd": 6,
      "target": "../sections/sec-001-the-counsellors-module.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "This module is for staff in clinical and other settings who counsel PrEP users. Those who counsel PrEP users may be lay, \npeer or professional counsellors and healthcare workers including nurses, clinical officers and doctors. \nThis module does not include information about community education; that is the focus of the module for community \neducators and advocates. Community education can shape awareness of and demand for PrEP, HIV testing and treatment. \nIf people receive accurate information about PrEP in the community, counselling may support plans for effective use, for \nexample promoting",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Defines the counsellors module as intended for lay, peer and professional counsellors and health workers who counsel PrEP users, distinguishing it from community education. It restates WHO's PrEP recommendation and that PrEP should not undermine condom programming or harm reduction.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-258517/blocks/prose-sec-002",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Counselling goals related to PrEP",
      "pageStart": 6,
      "pageEnd": 7,
      "target": "../sections/sec-002-counselling-goals-related-to-prep.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The goals of counselling depend on whether the client is starting PrEP or is already using PrEP. Counselling may explore \ncurrent sexual health and drug use protection plans and raise awareness about PrEP and other prevention strategies. \nMixing sexual health education with client-centred discussion (see below) can facilitate open communication about sexual \nhealth desires, goals and plans. Counsellors can help clients develop broader plans for fertility, pregnancy prevention and \nsexual health, and the ways to carry out these plans without acquiring sexually transmitted infections (STIs). Cou",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Outlines the goals of PrEP counselling at initiation and follow-up, including exploring sexual health and drug use, building prevention and fertility plans, and addressing adherence and disclosure challenges.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-258517/blocks/prose-sec-003",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "PrEP and “seasons of risk”",
      "pageStart": 7,
      "pageEnd": 7,
      "target": "../sections/sec-003-prep-and-seasons-of-risk.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "People often move in and out of \n“seasons of risk” and “risky \nsituations” for HIV infection.\nFor both antiretroviral therapy (ART) for HIV treatment and PrEP, adherence is \ncrucial. However, adherence to ART involves lifelong therapy with daily doses. In \ncontrast, among PrEP users, HIV exposure may be episodic. People often move in \nand out of “seasons of risk” and “risky situations” (1, 2). Thus, people taking \nPrEP will not take it all their lives. Learning how and when to start and stop PrEP \nis key to its effective use (3). PrEP counselling creates an opportunity for PrEP \nusers and thei",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that HIV exposure is often episodic, so PrEP users move through 'seasons of risk' and need to learn when to start and stop PrEP. It encourages counsellors to discuss with users how long they plan to use PrEP and how they will decide it is no longer needed.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-258517/blocks/prose-sec-004",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Who can counsel about PrEP?",
      "pageStart": 7,
      "pageEnd": 8,
      "target": "../sections/sec-004-who-can-counsel-about-prep.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Counsellors and health workers should be carefully trained on PrEP use before they counsel clients about PrEP. In addition, \nthe user’s preferences should be considered. Some users may prefer talking with a peer or lay counsellor, who has some \nshared experiences. Others may prefer counselling from a medically trained counsellor (for example, a nurse, doctor or \nclinical officer). Integrating training on PrEP counselling skills into current HIV testing training and other relevant curricula \ncan provide an opportunity to introduce PrEP in a range of settings such as primary health, STI and repr",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Discusses who can counsel on PrEP, stressing training, refresher training and respect for user preferences between peer, lay or medically trained counsellors. It supports a role for lay and peer counsellors under supportive supervision by trained professionals.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-258517/blocks/prose-sec-005",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Counselling strategies",
      "pageStart": 8,
      "pageEnd": 15,
      "target": "../sections/sec-005-counselling-strategies.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Specific information about PrEP in client-friendly language is available in the module for PrEP users. Topics covered \ninclude: “What is PrEP?”, ”How well does PrEP work?”, ”Who can consider using PrEP?”, ”Who cannot use PrEP?”, ”How \ndo I store the medicines?”, ”When and how do I take PrEP?”, ”How can I remember to take PrEP?”, ”What if I miss a \ndose?”, ”What if I have side-effects?”, ”What about PrEP, pregnancy and breastfeeding?”, and “When and how can I stop \nPrEP?”. This section describes the approaches to conveying those messages most appropriately and effectively.\nCulturally relevant g",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Sets out PrEP counselling strategies, beginning with culturally relevant group activities and support groups, with examples from trials in the United States and sub-Saharan Africa. It describes principles for individual counselling, such as cultural sensitivity, client-centredness, autonomy and attention to unmet needs, and lists related services like mental health and violence counselling. It covers concerns for specific groups including serodiscordant couples and transgender people. It ends with sample follow-up questions on social experiences, sexual behaviour, HIV test results and plans for staying HIV- and STI-negative.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
