    {
      "id": "library/10665-258517/blocks/prose-sec-006",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Differentiated care",
      "pageStart": 15,
      "pageEnd": 15,
      "target": "../sections/sec-006-differentiated-care.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "As with HIV and TB care, PrEP programmes could consider the potentially facilitating role of differentiated care. To date \nthere is little experience of differentiated care approaches in PrEP services, but this is something that could be explored as \nservices evolve and mature. PrEP programmes are comparatively new, and patterns of adherence and, thus, in many \nregions the need for intensive adherence support remains to be determined. Strategies to deliver PrEP can recognize that \nsome PrEP users need basic kinds of support and others need more intensive support to achieve and maintain effecti",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Suggests that PrEP programmes explore differentiated care, as in HIV and TB services, giving more intensive support to those who need it. It mentions ART approaches such as adherence clubs, streamlined visits and longer intervals between visits for users doing well.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-258517/blocks/prose-sec-007",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Monitoring efforts",
      "pageStart": 15,
      "pageEnd": 16,
      "target": "../sections/sec-007-monitoring-efforts.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "As noted, developing strategies and programmes that are tailored to the needs of the specific community or PrEP user \npopulation presents the best opportunity for high quality, client-centred care. The entry point into planning and \ndevelopment of this approach is familiarity with community needs. As part of continuous quality improvement cycles, \nprogrammes should consult with community stakeholders to determine the most acute needs and desires of PrEP users. \nProgrammes can prepare for or adapt to address these needs and monitor outcomes closely to determine if needs are in \nfact being addre",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Urges programmes to consult communities on PrEP users' needs and to monitor adherence, retention and stopping and restarting PrEP as part of quality improvement.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
